<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhaktapur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bhadgaon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhaktapur is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, about 13 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>. It was the capital of the Malla kings of the whole valley until the late 15th century and then of the independent Kingdom of Bhaktapur until the Gorkha conquest of 1769. Its Durbar Square, palace and temples, such as the five-storey Nyatapola, are part of the Kathmandu Valley World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhaktapur.jpg" alt="Bhaktapur" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saroj Pandey · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nyatpola_%26_Bhairav_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bhaktapur lies in the eastern corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> and is a major Newar settlement. Locally it is called Khwopa, and it was historically known as Bhadgaon. The name Bhaktapur, first attested in an inscription of 928, is widely held to be a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> rendering of the older Newar name Khōpring, from words meaning cooked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and village, which appears in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> inscription of 594. Folklore says the valley was once a lake, drained when the Bodhisattva Manjushri cut a gorge with his sword; the Swiss geologist Toni Hagen showed that it was indeed a lake, which began to drain about thirty thousand years ago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The 14th-century Gopal Raj Vamshavali credits Ananda Deva, who ruled from 1146 to 1167, with founding the city, probably by joining earlier settlements, and with making it the capital with a royal court, Tripura Rājkula. Shrines of eight Matrikas around the city and that of Tripura Sundari at the centre make the whole town a sacred mandala. As seat of government Bhaktapur attracted invasions, because divisions in the royal family brought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a> and the Khasa Kingdom. Rudra Malla married his sister to the king of Tirhut in the 1310s to end its attacks.</p><p>After Nepal Mandala divided into three kingdoms, Bhaktapur stayed a wealthy and powerful Newar kingdom, helped by its place on the India-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> trade route. Queen Ganga Devi, the only queen regnant, ruled jointly with her two sons, and under her it reached its territorial zenith. In 1769 the Gorkha Kingdom annexed Bhaktapur, which lost importance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a> and stayed isolated. After 1846 Dhir Shumsher Rana, as mayor, had many old palaces demolished and replaced with buildings in British style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bhaktapur is predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, and about 90 per cent of its people are Newar. UNESCO inscribed it as a World Heritage Site in 1979. The Nyatapola, a five-roofed pagoda completed in 1702 and commissioned by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupatindra-malla">Bhupatindra Malla</a>, stands in Taumadhi Square, and Durbar Square holds the palace complex and the stone temples of Vatsala Devi and Siddhi Lakshmi. The city has about 104 hitis, carved water spouts, and 50 artificial ponds, the two oldest built in 1118 and 1168.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhaktapur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Narayan Shah, who became king of Gorkha in 1743, set out to bring under his rule the many principalities of the central Himalaya, among them the western confederations known as the Chaubisi and Baisi kingdoms and the three Newar kingdoms of the valley: Kathmandu, Lalitpur (Patan) and <b>Bhaktapur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1768 Prithvi Narayan Shah captured Kathmandu, followed by Patan and <b>Bhaktapur</b>, and moved his capital there, creating the Kingdom of Nepal.</blockquote><a href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Raya Malla kept <b>Bhaktapur</b>, the old capital in the east of the valley, while his brothers established the Kingdom of Kantipur and other seats, creating rival Malla kingdoms in a small area.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a></li><li><blockquote>Prithvi Narayan Shah of the Gorkha Kingdom captured Patan in 1768, shortly after Kathmandu, and its last ruler Tej Narsingh Malla fled to <b>Bhaktapur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lalitpur">Kingdom of Lalitpur</a></li><li><blockquote>The early centuries were marked by raids from the Khasa Malla kingdom and the Karnat dynasty, and by rivalries between noble houses based at <b>Bhaktapur</b>, Patan and Kathmandu.</blockquote><a href="/state/malla-dynasty-nepal">Malla dynasty (Nepal)</a></li></ul></section>
